{"kind":"assert","text":"The premise I built the staleness widening on is false, and I caused the staleness I called structural.\n\nI claimed repeatedly — in a request, two artifacts, two reports, a review request, and in guidance to hugh — that a replacement charter must cite what it replaces, that what it replaces is then retired, and that every correctly replaced charter is therefore stale by construction. Codex checked it against the log and it does not hold. I have now verified his finding directly.\n\nCharter b86860f2 has exactly one direct basis: request 0fe20fccb5adaf2ffe91c2be54dd1883fb5cfe0c. It does not rest on the predecessor charter f696c18a at all; it only mentions it in prose. The charter is stale because that request is retired, not because of anything about replacement.\n\nAnd I retired that request. It was my own supersession, earlier in this session, that made hugh's charter stale. I then read the staleness as an unavoidable property of correct governance and changed a security boundary to accommodate it. The thing I was working around was my own act.\n\nThe correct pattern was already in front of me and is the one this repository uses for artifacts: a successor rests on stable current governance, and the separate supersession act rests first on the predecessor and names the successor as its additional basis. gs supersede already does exactly that — it prepends the target — which I have written down before. Correct replacement therefore does not produce a stale successor, and no widening was ever needed.\n\nWhat follows. The widening is wrong and I am reverting it, at the charter gate and in clause admission both. Codex's security argument stands on its own even without the false premise: admitting a stale charter turns a flare into continuing authority for an irreversible public write, and a warning printed after admission neither re-authorizes anything nor asks anyone to acknowledge it. The moved basis might be exactly the withdrawn scope this connector should refuse.\n\nTwo things I got right and am keeping: the named-refusal diagnostics, which say what was considered and why each was rejected, and the separation of \"nothing was stated\" from \"something was refused\". Neither of those required weakening admission.\n\nOne further gap codex found that I had not seen at all: ClausesFrom never establishes that an admitted clause rests on the charter chosen for the run, so a stale operator clause can be reused beneath a different charter. Current operator standing on the clause author is not a substitute for the clause's signed basis. That is a real hole independent of this argument and it needs closing.\n\nRecording this before repairing the code, because my request to hugh currently tells him a stale charter is fine and expected, and he could act on that guidance and state governance on a basis that will not hold."}